course: everyday
lesson: 200
title: Playing fair and cheating in games
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A game is only fun when everyone plays by the same rules. Cheating, like peeking in hide-and-seek, moving extra squares or changing the rules halfway because you are losing, spoils the game for everyone.
explain: Games rely on honesty: when you are tagged, you are «it», and when you are trusted with the bank in Monopoly, handling it honestly is a real test of character.
explain: If someone else cheats, a calm, kind remark like «I think you went one too many, let's count again» keeps the game fair without starting a fight.
explain: Not everything is cheating. Letting a small child win sometimes is kindness. A cheat code in a game only you play harms nobody, but showing off those scores as real would be a lie. Online hacks are different: they are unfair to other players, break the rules and often hide viruses.
explain: Example: a footballer in a Liverpool youth team falls over on purpose to win a free kick. That is called diving; it is cheating and can earn a yellow card.
fact: A game is only fun when everyone plays by the same rules.
fact: Agree on the rules before the game starts and stick to them; changing rules to win is cheating.
fact: Pointing out a mistake calmly keeps a game fair without starting a fight.
fact: Being trusted with the money or the score in a game is a test of honesty.
fact: Games rely on players being honest about what happened, like being tagged.
fact: Cheat codes in a game only you play harm nobody, but claiming those scores as real is lying.
fact: Hacks and cheat programs in online games are unfair, can get your account banned and often hide viruses.
fact: Everyone loses sometimes, and good losers are people others want to play with.
fact: Letting a small child win sometimes is kind, not cheating.
fact: Faking a foul in sport (diving) is cheating and can get a yellow card.
fact: Owning up when the referee missed something shows fair play, which people respect.
exercise: You are counting in hide-and-seek. What is fair? | Close your eyes and count properly | Peek a little so you know where to look | Count very fast and skip some numbers | Count with one eye open
exercise: A friend moves an extra square when he thinks nobody is looking. What is the best thing to say? | «I think you went one too many, let's count again» | «You're a cheat and I'm never ever playing this game with you again» | Nothing, and then cheat back yourself | «I'm telling everyone you cheat»
exercise: Your cousin changes the rules halfway because she is losing. What prevents this? | Agreeing on the rules before you start and sticking to them | Letting whoever is losing make new rules | Never playing games with cousins | Changing the rules back again when you are the one who is losing
exercise: You are the banker in Monopoly. What is right? | Handle the money honestly | Take a little extra, since it is only a game | Give your friends more money than others | Let the bank run out so the game ends
exercise: You are tagged but say you were not. What should you do instead? | Admit it and become «it» | Keep saying you were not tagged | Say the tag did not count because it was soft | Run away so nobody can tell
exercise: You use a cheat code in a single-player game. What is true? | It is your choice, but do not claim those scores as real | It is a serious crime to use any cheat code | It is fine to show the scores off to your friends as if they were real | It makes the game more fun for everyone else
exercise: Someone offers you a hack to win an online game. Why say no? | It is unfair, breaks the rules and can hide viruses | Hacks never work in online games | It would make the game far too easy, so you would get bored of it | Only adults are allowed to use hacks
exercise: You lose a game and want to throw the pieces. What is best? | Take a breath, say «good game» and try again | Throw them across the room, so everyone knows how upset you are | Say the winner must have cheated | Refuse to play anything ever again
exercise: An older sister lets her little brother win at snap. Is that cheating? | No, it is kind and keeps the game fun for him | Yes, losing on purpose is always cheating, whoever you are playing with | Yes, and she should be punished for it | No, because snap has no real rules
exercise: A player falls over on purpose to win a free kick. What is this? | Cheating, called diving, which can get a yellow card | A clever trick that good players use | Fine, as long as the referee does not see it happen on the pitch | Only cheating if the team loses anyway
exercise: Your team scored because the referee missed a handball. If asked, what is honest? | Own up, since fair play matters more than one goal | Deny it, since referees decide everything and the goal already counts | Say the other team handled it too | Say nothing and celebrate the goal
exercise: A friend keeps winning card games by looking at your cards. What should you do? | Hold your cards close and say kindly that peeking is not fair | Look at his cards too, to make it even | Stop being his friend without ever saying why, so he works it out himself | Throw the cards on the floor
test: A girl in Toronto notices the other team scored a point that should not count, in a game of rounders. What is the calm approach? | Say what she saw politely and suggest checking together | Shout that they are cheaters | Quit the game straight away without saying anything to anyone | Secretly add a point to her own team
test: A boy beat a video game using cheat codes and tells his class he finished it fairly. What is wrong? | Claiming the score as fair is a lie | Using cheat codes alone is against the law | Nothing, since only he played it | Video games do not count as games
test: Why would agreeing on rules before a game prevent arguments? | Nobody can change them halfway to win | Because rules are only for the winners | Because the loser gets to choose new rules | Because games with rules are always boring
test: A grandad lets his four-year-old granddaughter win at dominoes. What is this? | Kindness that keeps the game fun for a learner | Cheating that will spoil her forever and teach her to expect to win | A mistake he should apologise for | Proof that he is bad at dominoes
test: A website promises a free program to win every online match. What might happen if you use it? | Your account could be banned, and it might hide a virus | You will become the best player legally | Nothing, since such programs can never be detected by the game | The other players will thank you for it
test: Why does handling the bank honestly in a board game matter, if it is only a game? | Being trusted with something is a real test of honesty | Because the money in the game is real | Because the banker always wins anyway | It does not matter at all in a game
test: A girl loses a race at sports day. Which reaction makes others want to play with her? | Congratulating the winner and saying she will try again | Saying the winner started too early | Crying and refusing to race again | Saying the race was much too short to count and should be run again
test: In a game of tag in Sydney, Jack feels a light touch on his back. What is honest? | Admit he was tagged and become «it» | Say it was too soft to count | Say he was in a safe zone | Keep running so that nobody can be sure whether it really counted
test: we're losin so im changing the rules lol | That's cheating; stick to the rules you agreed at the start | Fine, it's your game | Fine if you're losing badly | Fine if you say it loudly
test: got tagged but nobody saw, should i say | Yes, own up, the game relies on honesty | No, nobody saw it, so it doesn't count as a real tag | Only if you were tagged twice | Say you weren't and keep running
test: let my 4yo cousin win snap, that cheating? | No, it's kind to let a small child win sometimes | Yes, it's the worst cheating | Yes, you must always try your hardest to win, whoever is playing | Only cheating if she notices
test: mates got a hack for the online game, gives free coins | Don't use it; it's unfair, can ban you and may hide viruses | Use it, everyone does | Use it once, it's fine | Use it on someone else's account, so that yours can't get banned
explain: A boy in Leeds cheated in a class game by peeking; when the others found out, nobody wanted him on their team for weeks.
explain: A girl in Ohio downloaded a cheat for an online game from a site that promised free gems; it put a virus on the family computer and her account was banned.
fact: Cheat downloads and "free" game hacks often carry viruses or steal accounts.
fact: People who cheat in games often get left out, because others do not trust them.
test: found a site with free hacks for my game | Don't download it, it can carry a virus and get you banned | Download it, everyone does | Download it on mum's computer | Only use it once
test: peeked at the cards, winning every round lol | Stop and play fair, or nobody will want to play with you | Keep doing it, winning is all | Peek more carefully | Blame others for peeking
test: mate cheated and won, gonna cheat back | Say it's not fair and play fair yourself | Cheat more than him | Quit and break the game | Tell him he's stupid
test: did u hear Ollie downloaded some free gems hack n it wiped his dad's whole laptop n they lost all their photos forever!! | Don't download game hacks, they often carry viruses; "forever" may be exaggerated | Free gems hacks are safe | Only phones get viruses | Download it if a friend did
test: apparently Lily peeks in hide n seek every time, everyone says she's a cheater n nobody should play with her, pass it on | Don't spread it; calmly say peeking isn't fair and play fair | Pass it on so she's left out | Peek too to get even | Cheating is fine in hide and seek
test: my cousin says a lad in his footy team dived n got a red card n banned for a year, is that real | Probably blown up, but diving is cheating and can earn a yellow card | Diving is clever and allowed | Divers always get banned for years | Referees never notice diving
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the alteration of rules halfway through a game in order to win? | It is cheating | It is a clever tactic | It is fair if one is losing | It is permitted once a game
exam: Identify the recommended response when another player is suspected of having moved too many squares. | A calm remark such as "let's count again" | Shouting that the player is a cheat | Quitting the game and leaving at once | Moving extra squares oneself in return
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning hacks and cheat programs in online games. | They are unfair, can get accounts banned and often hide viruses | They are harmless tools that make online games more fun for all | They are allowed, provided they are downloaded from a free site | They affect only the player who uses them and nobody else
exam: It is given that an adult allows a small child to win a game on occasion. Determine how this conduct is characterised. | It is kind, not cheating | It is cheating, and wrong | It is lying to the child | It breaks the game's rules
